    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A confederation of four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nahuas">Nahua</a> city-states east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valley-of-mexico">Valley of Mexico</a>, governed by its rulers and lords in council.</li><li>It resisted the Aztec <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aztec-empire">Triple Alliance</a> for generations despite the flower wars and a reported embargo on salt and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>.</li><li>Its alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> in 1519 supplied the warriors and support that brought down <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tenochtitlan">Tenochtitlan</a> in 1521.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> compared the government of Tlaxcala to those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, because, he wrote, it had no single supreme ruler.</p></aside>
    </section>

